Replace `<banned_user_id_1>`, `<banned_user_id_2>`, etc. With the actual IDs of the banned users. This query will return the total count of documents in the "users" collection where the "id" field does not match any of the provided banned user IDs. If you don't want to provide all the banned user IDs explicitly, you can also retrieve them from another collection using the $in and $or operators. Here's an example:

```js
db.getCollection("banned_users").find().map(function(user) {
    return user.id;
}).toArray(function(err, bannedIds) {
    db.users.count({ id: { $nin: bannedIds } })
});
```

This query retrieves the banned user IDs from the "banned_users" collection using the `map()` and `toArray()` methods, and then passes them as an array to the `$nin` operator in the final query to exclude those IDs. Note that the second query is asynchronous since it uses the callback-based approach. If you prefer to use the Promise-based or async/await syntax instead, you can modify it accordingly. Hope this helps! Let me know if you have any further questions.

