To implement the bubble sort algorithm for sorting an array of integers in ascending order, follow these steps:

1. Define a function to perform bubble sort:

```cpp
void bubbleSort(int arr[], int n) {
    bool swapped = true; // flag to check if any swap has been made in a pass
    int temp = 0; // temporary variable to store elements during swapping

    while (swapped) {
        swapped = false; // initialize flag to false
        for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
            if (arr[i] > arr[i+1]) { // compare adjacent elements
                temp = arr[i]; // store the greater element
                arr[i] = arr[i+1]; // replace the greater element with smaller one
                arr[i+1] = temp; // store the replaced element in a temporary variable
                swapped = true; // set flag to true as swap has been made
            }
        }
        n--; // decrement size of unsorted subarray
    }
}
```

2. To integrate this function into a larger program, say a job application system, you can define a JSON parser class that reads input JSON data, extracts the required fields, converts them to C++ data types, and passes them to the bubbleSort function. Similarly, after sorting the array, the sorted data can be written back to JSON format. Here's an example implementation:

